WEBVTT

00:00.160 --> 00:01.550
- [Instructor] In this
video we're going to dig

00:01.550 --> 00:04.030
a little bit deeper into our knowledge

00:04.030 --> 00:06.890
or our understanding of factoring.

00:06.890 --> 00:09.240
Now factoring is something
that we've been doing

00:09.240 --> 00:11.330
for many years now.

00:11.330 --> 00:13.660
You can go all the way back
to when you were thinking

00:13.660 --> 00:16.500
about how would I factor the number 12.

00:16.500 --> 00:21.000
Well I could write the number
12 as three times four.

00:21.000 --> 00:24.330
I could also write it as two times six.

00:24.330 --> 00:25.830
These are all legitimate factors.

00:25.830 --> 00:29.290
Or I could try to do a
prime factorization of 12,

00:29.290 --> 00:31.355
where I'm trying to
write it as the product

00:31.355 --> 00:34.650
of you could view it as its
most basic constituents,

00:34.650 --> 00:36.230
which would be the prime numbers.

00:36.230 --> 00:40.690
And so we've done stuff like,
well 12 can be expressed

00:40.690 --> 00:42.090
as two times six.

00:42.090 --> 00:44.820
Two is prime, but then
six can be expressed

00:44.820 --> 00:46.660
as two times three.

00:46.660 --> 00:51.140
And so 12 could be expressed
as two times two times three,

00:51.140 --> 00:52.370
which we see right over here.

00:52.370 --> 00:55.720
This is all review, and this
would be a prime factorization.

00:55.720 --> 00:57.540
And we saw an analogue

00:57.540 --> 00:59.790
when we first learned it in algebra I.

00:59.790 --> 01:03.180
In algebra I, we learned things like,

01:03.180 --> 01:05.170
and sometimes this might
be in a math I class

01:05.170 --> 01:07.070
or even in a pre-algebra class,

01:07.070 --> 01:10.148
you'll learn things like
hey, how do I factor

01:10.148 --> 01:13.770
x squared plus six x?

01:13.770 --> 01:15.320
And you might recognize that hey,

01:15.320 --> 01:18.470
x squared could be rewritten as x times x,

01:18.470 --> 01:22.220
and six x, that really
just means six times x.

01:22.220 --> 01:26.210
And so both of them have x as a factor,

01:26.210 --> 01:29.040
and so we might wanna factor that out.

01:29.040 --> 01:31.230
And so we could rewrite
this entire expression

01:31.230 --> 01:35.050
as x times x plus six.

01:35.050 --> 01:38.198
What we just did is we
factored out these x's

01:38.198 --> 01:40.920
that I am circling in blue.

01:40.920 --> 01:43.510
So in general, this idea of factoring,

01:43.510 --> 01:44.630
if you're thinking about numbers,

01:44.630 --> 01:46.840
you're writing one number as
the product of other numbers.

01:46.840 --> 01:48.260
If you're thinking about expressions,

01:48.260 --> 01:50.220
you're writing an
expression as the product

01:50.220 --> 01:51.920
of other expressions.

01:51.920 --> 01:54.770
Well now as we go a little bit
more advanced into algebra,

01:54.770 --> 01:56.330
we're gonna start
thinking about doing this

01:56.330 --> 01:58.390
with higher order expressions.

01:58.390 --> 02:01.610
So we've done it with just
an x or just an x squared,

02:01.610 --> 02:02.670
but now we're gonna start thinking about,

02:02.670 --> 02:04.500
well what happens if we have
something to the third power,

02:04.500 --> 02:08.500
fourth power, sixth power,
10th power, 100th power?

02:08.500 --> 02:10.650
But it's really the same ideas.

02:10.650 --> 02:13.280
And we could start with monomials,

02:13.280 --> 02:15.540
which is fancy word
for just a single term.

02:15.540 --> 02:20.130
So let's say I had something
like six x to the seventh.

02:20.130 --> 02:23.610
What are the different ways
that I could factor this?

02:23.610 --> 02:24.830
Pause this video and think about it.

02:24.830 --> 02:28.343
Can I express this as the
product of two other things?

02:29.190 --> 02:33.043
Well, I could rewrite this
as this as being equal to

02:33.043 --> 02:38.043
two x to the third times what?

02:38.480 --> 02:40.610
Well let's see, what do
I have to multiply two by

02:40.610 --> 02:41.443
to get to six?

02:41.443 --> 02:42.760
I have to multiply it by three.

02:42.760 --> 02:44.590
And what do I have to
multiply x to the third by

02:44.590 --> 02:45.660
to get to x to the seventh?

02:45.660 --> 02:48.810
I could multiply it times
three x to the fourth.

02:48.810 --> 02:50.860
Notice, two times three is six,

02:50.860 --> 02:54.100
x to the third times x to the
fourth is x to the seventh.

02:54.100 --> 02:56.440
We add exponents when we're multiplying,

02:56.440 --> 02:59.180
when we're multiplying
things with the same base.

02:59.180 --> 03:00.740
But this isn't the only way to factor.

03:00.740 --> 03:03.320
Just as we saw that three
times four wasn't the only way

03:03.320 --> 03:04.500
to factor 12.

03:04.500 --> 03:08.840
You could also express this
as maybe being equal to

03:08.840 --> 03:11.728
x to the sixth times what?

03:11.728 --> 03:15.570
Well, we would still have
to multiply by six then,

03:15.570 --> 03:17.830
and then we'd have to
multiply by another x.

03:17.830 --> 03:20.970
So we could write this as
x to the sixth times six x.

03:20.970 --> 03:24.530
So there is oftentimes
multiple ways to factor

03:24.530 --> 03:28.600
a higher degree monomial like this.

03:28.600 --> 03:31.700
And there is also an
analogue to doing something

03:31.700 --> 03:33.090
like a prime factorization.

03:33.090 --> 03:36.410
When you're trying to really decompose,

03:36.410 --> 03:40.030
rewrite this expression as a
product of it simplest parts.

03:40.030 --> 03:42.760
How would you do that
for six x to the seventh?

03:42.760 --> 03:44.670
Well, you could rewrite that.

03:44.670 --> 03:48.470
You could say six x to the seventh,

03:48.470 --> 03:51.020
well that's equal to, we could
think about the six first.

03:51.020 --> 03:53.340
We know that the prime
factorization of six

03:53.340 --> 03:56.280
is two times three, two times three.

03:56.280 --> 03:59.720
And then x to the
seventh is just seven x's

03:59.720 --> 04:00.950
multiplied by each other.

04:00.950 --> 04:04.300
So times x times x

04:04.300 --> 04:06.600
times x times x

04:06.600 --> 04:08.160
times x, how many is that?

04:08.160 --> 04:12.200
That's five, six, and seven.

04:12.200 --> 04:13.690
And so some of what we were doing,

04:13.690 --> 04:15.680
when I said two x to the third,

04:15.680 --> 04:16.920
what we really thought about is okay,

04:16.920 --> 04:20.550
I had a two, and then I
had x times x times x.

04:20.550 --> 04:22.370
And then what do I have to multiply that?

04:22.370 --> 04:27.370
Well I have to multiply that
by three x to the fourth.

04:27.690 --> 04:31.200
And as we will see, being
able to think about monomials

04:31.200 --> 04:34.380
in this way will be useful for factoring

04:34.380 --> 04:36.930
higher degree things
that aren't monomials,

04:36.930 --> 04:38.660
things that are binomials, trinomials,

04:38.660 --> 04:40.350
or polynomials in general.

04:40.350 --> 04:42.100
And we'll do that in future videos.
